```c
void UA_WorkQueue_manuallyProcessDelayed(UA_WorkQueue *wq) {
    UA_DelayedCallback *dc, *dc_tmp;
    SIMPLEQ_FOREACH_SAFE(dc, &wq->delayedCallbacks, next, dc_tmp) {
        SIMPLEQ_REMOVE_HEAD(&wq->delayedCallbacks, next);
        if(dc->callback)
            dc->callback(dc->application, dc->data);
        UA_free(dc);
    }
}
```

The `SIMPLEQ_FOREACH_SAFE` saves `dc_tmp = dc->next.sqe_next` before the loop body. Then after the loop body, `dc = dc_tmp`. 

But what if the callback (`dc->callback`) itself adds a new delayed callback to the HEAD of the list? Let me check `UA_WorkQueue_enqueueDelayed`:

```c
void UA_WorkQueue_enqueueDelayed(UA_WorkQueue *wq, UA_DelayedCallback *cb) {
    SIMPLEQ_INSERT_HEAD(&wq->delayedCallbacks, cb, next);
    ...
}
```

It inserts at the HEAD. So if the callback adds a new delayed callback, it becomes the new head. But `SIMPLEQ_FOREACH_SAFE` already saved the next pointer, so it would skip the newly added element and continue with the saved next. That's fine.

